tary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"Paper-2"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ca24m4-written-2a","question_number":"2a","question_type":"written","ib_level":"SL","source":{"course":"9702","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"March","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"42","paper_label":"42","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["B.3"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"Explain the internal energy of an ideal gas in terms of the random kinetic energy and intermolecular potential energy of its molecules.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"It is the total random kinetic energy of the molecules. Intermolecular forces are neglected in the ideal-gas model, so the intermolecular potential-energy contribution is taken as zero.","source_id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","evidence":{"question":"2a","pages":[7],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","board":"cambridge","course":"9702","year":2024,"session":"March","paper":"42","sha256":"ea108d62044d9875829c5d40370d45642d78f217f68b9f48a5b8a81d7787b05c","source_url":"https://pastpapers.papacambridge.com/directories/CAIE/CAIE-pastpapers/upload/9702_m24_ms_42.pdf","local_path":"papers/cambridge/9702/2024/9702_m24_ms_42--ea108d6204.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"9702","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"42"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ca24m4-written-2bii","question_number":"2bii","question_type":"written","ib_level":"SL","source":{"course":"9702","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"March","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"42","paper_label":"42","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["B.3"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"A fixed amount of ideal gas initially occupies 1.24 × 10⁻⁴ m³ at 20 °C. It expands at constant pressure by 5.20 × 10⁻⁵ m³. Calculate its final temperature in kelvin.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"T₁ = 293 K and V₂ = 1.76 × 10⁻⁴ m³. At constant pressure V/T is constant, so T₂ = 293 × 1.76/1.24 = 416 K.","source_id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","evidence":{"question":"2bii","pages":[7],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"cambridge-a2643bb4c378f550","board":"cambridge","course":"9702","year":2024,"session":"March","paper":"42","sha256":"ea108d62044d9875829c5d40370d45642d78f217f68b9f48a5b8a81d7787b05c","source_url":"https://pastpapers.papacambridge.com/directories/CAIE/CAIE-pastpapers/upload/9702_m24_ms_42.pdf","local_path":"papers/cambridge/9702/2024/9702_m24_ms_42--ea108d6204.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"9702","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"42"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]},{"id":"cross-ap24p2-written-2a","question_number":"2a","question_type":"written","ib_level":"SL","source":{"course":"Physics-2","level":"external","year":2024,"session":"May","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","paper":"FRQ","paper_label":"FRQ","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025"},"parts":[{"label":"whole","marks":null,"review_status":"reviewed","subtopic_ids":["B.3"],"depends_on":[],"context_ids":[],"display_text":"A fixed ideal-gas sample is sealed in a rigid chamber with a heater, temperature sensor and pressure sensor. Describe how to determine the number of molecules N experimentally. Include the measurements, analysis and a way to reduce uncertainty. Use Boltzmann constant kB.","assets":[],"answer":{"text":"Measure the gas volume V, allowing for any significant volume occupied by equipment. Record pressure p and absolute temperature T after allowing the sample to equilibrate at several heater settings. Plot p against T; the gradient s = NkB/V, so N = sV/kB. Repeat readings and use a best-fit line over a wide safe temperature range.","source_id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","evidence":{"question":"2a","pages":[5],"review":"Worked answer checked against the source marking guidance; wording adapted for independent IB practice."},"source":{"id":"ap-70c3b0898aaf42d1","board":"ap","course":"Physics-2","year":2024,"session":"May","paper":"FRQ","sha256":"4802c810bb75bc6b0618fec0654d69e4c29e1162c58c0e187be6f8e8d70c420a","source_url":"https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2026/02/4199-ap24-sg-physics-2.pdf","local_path":"papers/ap/Physics-2/2024/ap24-sg-physics-2--4802c810bb.pdf","level":"external","qualification":"Physics-2","collection":"supplementary","collection_label":"Extra question banks","syllabus":"IB Physics, first assessment 2025","paper_label":"FRQ"}},"given_text":null}],"contexts":[]}],"guidance":{"entries":{"duplicate-ec30a4d076b864e5":{"hint":"Link the macroscopic quantity to the random motion and wall collisions of the molecules.","notes":["Mean translational kinetic energy is 3kT/2 per molecule. The rms speed obeys v_rms = √(3kT/m), so molecular mass matters when comparing gases.","Pressure comes from the rate of momentum transfer to container walls. In the ideal model, particles have negligible volume and negligible interactions except during elastic collisions."]},"fep-bee05cf16677a325-q14:whole":{"hint":"Identify what stays fixed: amount of gas, temperature, pressure or volume. Use only the simplification that those conditions allow.","notes":["Use pV = nRT = NkT with absolute pressure and kelvin temperature. For a fixed amount, p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂.","If gas leaks or moves between containers, the amount in one container may change. Apply conservation of total molecules to the complete closed system instead of assuming pV/T is fixed locally."]},"duplicate-1b92817c41190c65":{"hint":"Identify what stays fixed: amount of gas, temperature, pressure or volume. Use only the simplification that those conditions allow.","notes":["Use pV = nRT = NkT with absolute pressure and kelvin temperature. For a fixed amount, p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂.","If gas leaks or moves between containers, the amount in one container may change. Apply conservation of total molecules to the complete closed system instead of assuming pV/T is fixed locally."]},"duplicate-47189987565313e7":{"hint":"Identify what stays fixed: amount of gas, temperature, pressure or volume. Use only the simplification that those conditions allow.","notes":["Use pV = nRT = NkT with absolute pressure and kelvin temperature. For a fixed amount, p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂.","If gas leaks or moves between containers, the amount in one container may change. Apply conservation of total molecules to the complete closed system instead of assuming pV/T is fixed locally."]},"duplicate-bc4d8239cbc6a422":{"hint":"Think about both the rate of wall collisions and the momentum change in each collision.","notes":["Gas pressure is momentum transferred to the walls per unit time per unit area. Higher temperature increases typical molecular speeds and momentum transfer.","A smaller volume also increases collision frequency with the walls. Collisions between molecules alone are not the direct force exerted on a wall.","Link the relevant physical cause to the observation asked about. Naming a law alone does not explain why this particular result follows."]},"duplicate-8c5fa96582e18f78":{"hint":"Identify what stays fixed: amount of gas, temperature, pressure or volume. Use only the simplification that those conditions allow.","notes":["Use pV = nRT = NkT with absolute pressure and kelvin temperature. For a fixed amount, p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂.","If gas leaks or moves between containers, the amount in one container may change. Apply conservation of total molecules to the complete closed system instead of assuming pV/T is fixed locally."]},"fep-0173f6e17d4c6961-q13:whole":{"hint":"Identify what stays fixed: amount of gas, temperature, pressure or volume. Use only the simplification that those conditions allow.","notes":["Use pV = nRT = NkT with absolute pressure and kelvin temperature. For a fixed amount, p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂.","If gas leaks or moves between containers, the amount in one container may change. Apply conservation of total molecules to the complete closed system instead of assuming pV/T is fixed locally."]},"cross-ca23j1-14:whole":{"hint":"Pressure compares the force on a surface with the size of that surface.","notes":["Pressure = normal force / area. Its SI unit is the pascal, equivalent to N m⁻². Do not confuse pressure with density, which is mass per volume."],"method":"gas-pressure-model","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ed24u5-8:whole":{"hint":"Link the macroscopic quantity to the random motion and wall collisions of the molecules.","notes":["Mean translational kinetic energy is 3kT/2 per molecule. The rms speed obeys v_rms = √(3kT/m), so molecular mass matters when comparing gases.","Pressure comes from the rate of momentum transfer to container walls. In the ideal model, particles have negligible volume and negligible interactions except during elastic collisions."],"method":"gas-kinetic","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-aq24p2-9:whole":{"hint":"Link the macroscopic quantity to the random motion and wall collisions of the molecules.","notes":["Mean translational kinetic energy is 3kT/2 per molecule. The rms speed obeys v_rms = √(3kT/m), so molecular mass matters when comparing gases.","Pressure comes from the rate of momentum transfer to container walls. In the ideal model, particles have negligible volume and negligible interactions except during elastic collisions."],"method":"gas-kinetic","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-2a:whole":{"hint":"Which interaction is neglected in the ideal-gas model?","notes":["Internal energy concerns microscopic random motion, not the bulk motion of the container. The absence of an intermolecular potential contribution is an ideal-gas assumption."],"method":"gas-kinetic","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ca24m4-written-2bii:whole":{"hint":"First distinguish the volume increase from the final volume.","notes":["Gas-law ratios require absolute temperature in kelvin. Add the volume increase before forming V₂/V₁; using 20 °C in the ratio is invalid."],"method":"ideal-gas","review_status":"reviewed"},"cross-ap24p2-written-2a:whole":{"hint":"Which gas-law quantities remain constant while the heater changes the temperature?","notes":["For fixed N and V, p = (NkB/V)T. Identify the gradient algebraically instead of treating one noisy reading as exact. Use kelvin and absolute pressure."],"method":"ideal-gas","review_status":"reviewed"}}},"answers":{"entries":{"fep-48711ac5ebc72e6f-q17:whole":{"status":"source-key-located","text":"Correct option: A.","assets":[],"source_id":"fep-4265fcfd242841b2","copied_from":"fep-346916a021267156-q17:whole"}}},"presentation":{"practice/parts/fep-3f5e391adea92c25-q17-whole-75737e533ea2711e.webp":{"crop":[0,6,1051,429],"size":[1051,489],"masks":[[14,5,33,35]]},"practice/parts/fep-bee05cf16677a325-q14-whole-85341db987a7f98d.webp":{"crop":[0,6,1051,269],"size":[1051,346],"masks":[[14,5,33,35]]},"practice/parts/fep-bee05cf16677a325-q16-whole-8d381ea1059cb6dd.webp":{"crop":[0,6,1051,503],"size":[1051,580],"masks":[[14,5,33,35]]},"practice/parts/fep-48711ac5ebc72e6f-q17-whole-a6ee09a6552a7ac5.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1072,261],"size":[1072,297],"masks":[[7,5,33,29]]},"practice/parts/fep-8337a8ecd9829ef5-q15-whole-e1fb2ee4faa6917d.webp":{"crop":[0,3,1094,492],"size":[1094,719],"masks":[[7,5,33,29]]},"practice/parts/fep-a38d101e37552b14-q14-whole-a0d730dc0e5dce7b.webp"